diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist in the live lab. A thorough examination of every layer of the simple_bgp topology — control plane, routing table, data plane, interface state, and configuration files — shows the network is fully healthy.

The BGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is in the Established state, has been stable since lab startup, and is exchanging exactly the expected two prefixes: router2 correctly originates and advertises 200.1.1.0/24 to router1, and router1 installs it as the best BGP route (`B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2`). The return path is equally healthy — router1 advertises 195.11.14.0/24 to router2, which installs it as `B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1`. All four interfaces (router1 eth0/eth1, router2 eth0/eth1) are UP with no link flaps recorded. Neither router has any outbound or inbound prefix-list, route-map, or filter of any kind applied to the BGP neighbor.

A live data-plane probe confirms the alleged symptom is not reproducible: a 4-packet ICMP test from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) succeeds with 0% packet loss and ~1 ms round-trip time. The startup FRR configuration files match the running configuration on both routers with no discrepancy.

The helpdesk ticket's description — "their addresses have gone missing from our list" — likely describes the normal BGP convergence window of a few seconds that occurs every time the lab is first started, not a persistent or injected fault. No corrective action is required; the network is operating as designed.
=== END ===